<CtxMethod id="msg.reply.text" />

<CtxMethod id="send.to" />

The options accepted by each method (`text`, `image`/`video`/`gif`, `audio`, `poll`, `sticker`/`file`)
are gathered in [ctx — options and signatures](/docs/api/ctx-options/#ctxsend).

> `ctx.send.gif()` (and `ctx.msg.reply.gif()`) automatically converts the file to `mp4` via
> `ffmpeg` before sending — WhatsApp doesn't accept a real GIF as media.

### Post-send actions (chaining)

```js
await ctx.send.text("Done!").react("✅");

const handle = await ctx.send.image("/tmp/x.jpg"); // handle = the sent Message
await handle.delete();
```

`delete(forEveryone? = true)` and `react(emoji)` return a `Promise`. Currently, `delete(false)`
("delete for me only") **does nothing** — only `delete()`/`delete(true)` (delete for everyone)
actually has an effect.

```js
const handle = await ctx.send.text("Processing...");
await handle.edit("Done! ✅");
```

`edit(text)` only works on messages sent **by the bot itself** — calling it on a message that isn't
`fromMe` throws an error. With `SECURITY_LEVEL = "high"` (`manybot.toml`), edits go through a
throttle (like the rest of sends); on the other levels (`low`/`medium`) no throttle at all
is applied to `edit()`.

> `pin(duration?)` also exists in the interface, but currently **has no effect** — Baileys (the
> library behind the WhatsApp connection) doesn't support pinning messages yet, so calling
> `.pin()` just logs a warning and does nothing. We left the method there for when that changes.

> **send vs reply:** `ctx.send.*` sends without quoting anything. `ctx.msg.reply.*` quotes the message that
> triggered the handler — prefer `reply` in groups. See [ctx.msg](/docs/api/ctx-msg/).

> **`ctx.send.to()` inside a handler** doesn't apply your plugin's `guardOptions` `cooldown`/`jitter`
> (only the global send limit still applies) — that's because `.to()` is normally meant to
> notify another chat, not the current one. In `setup()`, on the other hand, `ctx.send.to()` applies
> `cooldown`/`jitter` normally (the defaults, `true`/`true`, since there's no chat
> `guardOptions` at that point).

> ⚠️ **Experimental — part of the [new `commands.yaml` architecture](/docs/commands-yaml/) 🧪,
> still being tested and not 100% functional. Subject to change without notice in `5.x` versions.**

Exclusive chat lock (runtime only — it doesn't exist in `setup()`, since there's no current chat to
lock at that point), scoped to the current chat **and** the plugin that called it. Meant for
interactive flows (games, a sticker session with a timeout, a multi-step download prompt)
that can't have two plugins competing for the same chat at the same time.

The kernel only controls **who** holds the lock — all of the session's actual state (timeout, collected
media, whose turn it is, etc.) remains the plugin's responsibility.

```js
export default async function (ctx) {
  if (ctx.msg.is("start-game")) {
    if (!ctx.session.acquire()) {
      return void await ctx.msg.reply.text("There's already another active session in this chat.");
    }
    await ctx.msg.reply.text("Session started!");
    return;
  }

  if (ctx.session.isMine()) {
    // process the move, session message, etc.
  }

  if (ctx.msg.is("exit")) {
    ctx.session.release();
    await ctx.msg.reply.text("Session ended.");
  }
}
```

| Method | Description |
|---|---|
| `acquire()` | Opens the session for this plugin in this chat. `true` if it succeeded (or if this same plugin already owned it — safe to call again on a later message in the same flow); `false` if another plugin already holds the lock. |
| `release()` | Releases the session — only has an effect if this plugin is the one holding it. |
| `isLocked()` | Whether the current chat has any open session (from any plugin). |
| `isMine()` | Whether **this** plugin is the one holding the current chat's session. |

Per-chat (or global) settings storage, persisted to disk — think of it as "preferences
the user adjusts from WhatsApp itself", different from [`ctx.storage`](/docs/api/ctx-storage/)
(the plugin's free-form data).

```js
// in the current chat
ctx.settings.set("welcome", true);
const enabled = ctx.settings.get("welcome", false); // with fallback
ctx.settings.getAll();      // all keys for this chat
ctx.settings.delete("welcome");
ctx.settings.deleteAll();

// bot-wide setting, not tied to any chat
ctx.settings.global.set("maintenanceMode", true);
ctx.settings.global.get("maintenanceMode", false);

// setting for another specific chat
ctx.settings.forChat(otherChatId).set("language", "en");
```

### Communities

Groups that belong to the same WhatsApp community can share settings:

```js
ctx.settings.link(communityId);          // links the current chat to a community
ctx.settings.unlink();                   // removes the current chat's link
ctx.settings.getCommunityId();           // string | null
ctx.settings.getCommunityChats();        // string[] — chats linked to the same community
```

See the full method table in [ctx — options and signatures](/docs/api/ctx-options/#ctxsettings).

> In `setup()`, only `ctx.settings.global` is available — with no current chat, the methods that
> operate on the "current chat" (including `forChat`/`link`/`unlink`) don't make sense there.

The plugin's persistent data directory (`~/.manybot/data/<key>/`), created automatically.

```js
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, existsSync } from "fs";

const dbPath = ctx.storage.resolve("data.json"); // creates subfolders if needed
const data = existsSync(dbPath) ? JSON.parse(readFileSync(dbPath, "utf-8")) : {};
data[ctx.msg.sender] = Date.now();
writeFileSync(dbPath, JSON.stringify(data, null, 2));
```

See `dir` and `resolve()` in [ctx — options and signatures](/docs/api/ctx-options/#ctxstorage).

> Survives reinstalls. `manyplug remove` asks before deleting it (`-Y` skips all prompts).
> `resolve()` rejects attempts to escape the directory (`../`, absolute paths) — it always
> returns a path inside `dir`.
